  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/nerita-polita-2/",
      "html": "<p>Nerita polita, the Polished Nerite or \"racing snail\", is an algae-eating snail. Shell size varies from 13–40 mm; the specimens obtained were about 10 mm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/nerita-polita-2/",
      "html": "<p>The species inhabits large parts of the Indo-West-Pacific and is extremely common. The specimens were obtained from Thailand. It was described scientifically in 1758 by Carl von Linné under the name that is still valid today.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/nerita-polita-2/",
      "html": "<p>Basically, Nerita polita is a marine species. But it lives in the uppermost coastline and will occasionally fall dry during low tide. If it starts raining during that time, the snail finds itself from one moment to the next in pure freshwater.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/nerita-polita-2/",
      "html": "<p>It is quite important to cover the tank closely, because the snails follow their inner tidal clock from time to time and leave the water.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/nerita-polita-2/",
      "html": "<p>Successful keeping of N. polita is possible at any salt content from pure freshwater to pure saltwater. However, the pH must not sink below 8 for longer periods, as this is not tolerated.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/nerita-polita-2/",
      "html": "<p>In freshwater aquaria there is no need to fear a snail plague, because Nerita snails reproduce via a free-swimming larva that needs pure saltwater to survive. As far as is known, there are no reports of successful breeding of this species under aquarium conditions.</p>"
    }
  ],
